63. What is crosstalk?
Crosstalk is the unwanted interference or noise that occurs when a signal in one wire (aggressor) affects the signal in a nearby wire (victim) due to capacitive and inductive coupling.

64. Why does crosstalk occur? 
Crosstalk occurs because two signal wires are placed close to each other. When the signal on one wire changes, it creates electric and magnetic fields that couple into the neighboring wire. This unwanted coupling transfers noise from one wire to the other.

65. What is an aggressor? 
An aggressor is the signal wire that causes crosstalk. It is the wire whose changing voltage or current creates electric and magnetic fields, inducing unwanted noise in a nearby wire.

66. What is a victim? 
The nearby wire affected by this noise is called the victim.

67. What is coupling capacitance? 
Coupling capacitance is the parasitic capacitance that exists between two nearby signal wires. When the voltage on one wire changes, this capacitance can transfer part of the signal to the neighboring wire, causing crosstalk.

68. What is crosstalk noise? 
Crosstalk noise is the unwanted voltage (noise) induced on a victim wire due to the switching activity of a nearby aggressor wire. It is mainly caused by coupling capacitance between adjacent wires.

69. What is crosstalk delay? 
Crosstalk delay is the increase or decrease in the propagation delay of a victim

70. What happens when aggressor and victim switch in the same direction? 
When the aggressor and victim switch in the same direction, the coupling effect is reduced.

71. What happens when aggressor and victim switch in opposite directions? 
When the aggressor and victim switch in opposite directions, the coupling effect increases.

72. How can crosstalk affect setup and hold timing? 
1. Effect on Setup Timing

For setup timing, the data must reach the capture flip-flop before the setup deadline.

If crosstalk slows down the data transition, the data arrival time increases.
This can cause setup violation.
Therefore, crosstalk can make setup timing worse.

2. Effect on Hold Timing

For hold timing, the data must not arrive too early after the clock edge.

If crosstalk speeds up the data path, data can arrive earlier.
This reduces hold slack and can cause a hold violation.
Therefore, crosstalk can make hold timing worse when it causes the data path to become faster.


